---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import "@/assets/css/main.css";

import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import GeorgiaHome from "@/assets/images/georgia-home.png";
---

<Layout>
  <main class="georgia-project">
    <div class="wrap top">
      <a class="back" href="/projects">&larr; Back to all projects</a>

      <h1>Georgia Tours — Tour Booking, Payments &amp; SEO Enhancements</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A WordPress tour-booking website built around WP Travel Engine, with
        custom development focused on payment integration, international price
        presentation, trip-page UX, language selection, and search visibility.
        The work brings together booking-engine customization, theme-level
        layout changes, and SEO enhancements for a travel business promoting
        experiences in Georgia.
      </p>

      <dl class="meta">
        <div>
          <dt>Project</dt>
          <dd>Georgia Tours</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>My role</dt>
          <dd>
            WordPress development, Payment integrations, custom plugin
            development, debugging, technical SEO
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Year</dt>
          <dd>2026</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Platform</dt>
          <dd>WordPress · WP Travel Engine · Tourex</dd>

          <dt>
            <a href="https://georgia-tours.org" rel="noopener" target="_blank"
              >Visit Georgia Tours</a
            >
          </dt>
        </div>
        <dd></dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </main>

  <div class="wrap">
    <figure class="shot wide hero-shot">
      <div class="bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
      <Image src={GeorgiaHome} alt="R&P Consulting homepage" loading="eager" />
      <figcaption>
        Project context: a travel-booking experience for tours in Georgia.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <div class="narrow body-copy">
        <h2>The challenge</h2>
        <p>
          A tour website needs to do more than display attractive destinations.
          Visitors must be able to understand each trip, compare prices, choose
          a booking option, and move through checkout with confidence. Behind
          that experience, the site also needs reliable payment handling,
          consistent trip metadata, and a layout that makes the most important
          booking information easy to find.
        </p>
        <p>
          The project involved extending an existing WordPress setup rather than
          replacing its booking engine. That meant working with WP Travel
          Engine's trip post type and hooks, the Tourex theme's templates, and
          Yoast SEO's existing metadata output without creating unnecessary
          conflicts or duplicate functionality.
        </p>
        <p>
          A key constraint was to keep the booking system's actual transaction
          currency separate from any converted prices shown for convenience.
          Payment initiation, checkout display, and confirmed payment status had
          to remain distinct parts of the flow.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What I worked on</h2>
      <div class="feats">
        <div>
          <h3>Flitt payment gateway integration</h3>
          <p>
            I built a plugin that allowed adding Flitt as a selectable payment
            method in the WP Travel Engine checkout, including gateway settings
            and the embedded-checkout flow.
          </p>
          <p>
            The integration work included tracing order creation, checking
            credential configuration, and correcting the checkout-token endpoint
            used to open the payment interface.
          </p>
          <p>
            From all this, the plugin also created bookings and sent the
            appropriate emails and notifications to the admin and clients who
            booked the trip.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Checkout request and response handling</h3>
          <p>
            Investigated the Create Order response and its signature
            verification logic. The implementation needs to validate each
            response according to the payment provider's contract rather than
            assume every response contains the same signature fields. A created
            order or opened checkout is not treated as proof of a successful
            payment.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Multi-currency price display planning</h3>
          <p>
            Created a display-only currency switcher plugin for GEL, USD, EUR,
            and GBP, using USD as the booking system's base currency.
          </p>
          <p>
            The experience is intended to show indicative converted prices while
            clearly explaining that checkout continues to charge in the
            configured base currency.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Trip-page layout debugging</h3>
          <p>
            Traced the single-trip content flow through WP Travel Engine hooks
            and examined why the intended main-content-plus-sidebar layout was
            collapsing into one column.
          </p>
          <p>
            The work focused on hook priorities and the surrounding
            theme/template structure instead of assuming that reordering
            callbacks alone would fix the layout.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>SEO metadata and social sharing</h3>
          <p>
            Reviewed how Yoast SEO and the theme contribute trip titles,
            descriptions, and Open Graph metadata. The goal is to provide useful
            trip-specific snippets and sharing previews while avoiding duplicate
            tags from multiple layers of the WordPress stack.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>FAQ content and structured data</h3>
          <p>
            Planned an FAQ structure using the ACF fields
            <code>faq_question</code> and <code>faq_answer</code>, with
            corresponding FAQPage JSON-LD where appropriate.
          </p>
          <p>
            Any generated schema matched the questions and answers visible on
            the page and avoided duplicating schema already emitted by other
            components.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Tour offer structured data</h3>
          <p>
            Evaluated the need for consistent Product/Offer-style structured
            data around tour pricing. The implementation reflected the actual
            offer and transaction currency, and accounted for the trip schema
            already available through the booking plugin before adding custom
            JSON-LD.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Theme and plugin integration</h3>
          <p>
            Worked within an existing WordPress stack rather than treating every
            requirement as a separate application.
          </p>
          <p>
            The solution considers WP Travel Engine hooks, Tourex templates,
            Yoast output, ACF fields, and Elementor as connected parts of one
            visitor experience.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="pair">
        <figure class="shot half">
          <div class="bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
          <div class="concept-panel">
            <span class="panel-label">Booking experience</span>
            <h3>Trip details → booking → payment</h3>
            <div class="flow-step">
              <span>01</span><div>
                <strong>Explore a trip</strong><small
                  >Duration, itinerary and key facts</small
                >
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flow-step">
              <span>02</span><div>
                <strong>Review the price</strong><small
                  >Clear base currency and display price</small
                >
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flow-step">
              <span>03</span><div>
                <strong>Choose payment</strong><small
                  >Gateway selection in checkout</small
                >
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flow-step">
              <span>04</span><div>
                <strong>Confirm the outcome</strong><small
                  >Booking status follows verified payment state</small
                >
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            Booking flow and payment-state considerations.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="shot half">
          <div class="bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
          <div class="concept-panel">
            <span class="panel-label">Technical SEO</span>
            <h3>Trip metadata built around the actual offer</h3>
            <div class="schema-row">
              <span>Title</span><strong>Trip name + destination</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="schema-row">
              <span>Description</span><strong
                >Duration, location and booking context</strong
              >
            </div>
            <div class="schema-row">
              <span>FAQ</span><strong>ACF question-and-answer fields</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="schema-row">
              <span>Offer</span><strong>Price and currency consistency</strong>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            SEO and structured-data design considerations.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="narrow body-copy process">
      <h2>How it's built</h2>

      <h3>Working with the WordPress booking engine</h3>
      <p>
        WP Travel Engine provides the core trip and booking functionality. The
        trip content type uses the <code>trip</code> slug, and the customization work
        builds around the plugin's existing hooks and data model. This approach keeps
        trip management within WordPress and reduces the need to recreate booking
        features that the plugin already provides.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Tourex theme controls important parts of the presentation layer.
        When a trip page does not render in the intended layout, both the hook
        callbacks and the template's parent containers are used to creat the
        intended layout. Hook priority determines callback order but does not
        independently create a two-column layout or repair an incompatible
        wrapper.
      </p>

      <h3>Flitt checkout integration</h3>
      <p>
        The payment work separates order creation from the embedded checkout
        step and from final payment confirmation. The checkout flow uses the
        provider's token endpoint to initialize the embedded payment interface.
      </p>
      <p>
        The token endpoint was distinguished from a separate checkout URL
        endpoint, and the Create Order response was reviewed against the
        signature checks applied by the integration.
      </p>
      <p>
        A successful checkout initialization alone is not sufficient evidence
        that the live payment flow, callbacks, and booking-status updates are
        all working correctly, so I completed automated and manual end-to-end
        verification for those stages.
      </p>

      <h3>Currency display without changing the charge</h3>
      <p>
        The currency-switcher was built to be intentionally display-only. The
        planned currencies were Georgian lari (GEL), US dollars (USD), euros
        (EUR), and British pounds (GBP), with USD retained as the booking
        system's base currency. Frankfurter was used as the exchange-rate
        source, and the switcher was placed in the Elementor header as well as a
        custom footer section only show on mobile.
      </p>
      <p>
        A modal was used to explain that converted values are indicative and
        that the final booking is charged in the base currency. This boundary
        matters because changing a display preference must never silently change
        the amount or currency sent to the payment gateway.
      </p>

      <h3>SEO, FAQs, and structured data</h3>
      <p>
        Yoast SEO was already participating in title and metadata generation, so
        custom SEO code needed to work with that output.
      </p>
      <p>
        The FAQ implementation uses ACF fields named <code>faq_question</code> and
        <code>faq_answer</code>. FAQPage JSON-LD was and ontinues to be
        generated from the same content users can see on the page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Product/Offer-style schema also reflected the actual tour offer,
        including the correct price and currency. Since WP Travel Engine can
        emit trip-related schema, its existing output was inspected before
        custom structured data wass added. The aim was consistent,
        non-duplicated metadata rather than simply adding more JSON-LD.
      </p>

      <h3>Maintainability and debugging</h3>
      <p>
        The implementation uses WordPress-native extension points where
        possible, including plugin settings, hooks, filters, custom fields, and
        theme templates. PHP development tooling and WordPress/WP Travel Engine
        stubs were also part of the local workflow to improve editor completion
        and catch integration issues during development.
      </p>

      <ul class="tags">
        <li>WordPress</li>
        <li>PHP</li>
        <li>WP Travel Engine</li>
        <li>Flitt</li>
        <li>Yoast SEO</li>
        <li>Advanced Custom Fields</li>
        <li>Elementor</li>
        <li>JSON-LD</li>
        <li>Frankfurter API</li>
        <li>WordPress hooks</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Project outcomes</h2>
      <div class="results">
        <div class="res">
          <strong>Payment flow</strong>
          <span
            >Embedded checkout endpoint and response-validation during live
            payments.</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="res">
          <strong>Currency clarity</strong>
          <span
            >A defined display-only conversion approach that keeps the base
            booking currency authoritative.</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="res">
          <strong>SEO foundation</strong>
          <span
            >Trip metadata, ACF-powered FAQs, Open Graph, and structured-data
            requirements implemented.</span
          >
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap next">
      <a class="proj" href="/projects/ShuleYetu"
        ><small>Next project</small><span class="t"
          >ShuleYetu school management</span
        ></a
      >
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/projects">View all work</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="contact">
    <div class="wrap cta">
      <h2>Need a booking website or a custom WordPress integration?</h2>
      <p>
        I build and extend websites with a focus on reliable workflows,
        maintainable code, and a clear user experience.
      </p>
      <a class="btn" href="mailto:kengitahi@proton.me?subject=Project%20enquiry"
        >Start a project</a
      >
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .hero-kicker,
  .hero-overlay > span:last-child {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .concept-panel {
    min-height: 330px;
    padding: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    color: var(--text, #222);
    background: var(--surface, #f7f7f5);
  }

  .panel-label {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 0.65rem;
    color: var(--muted, #666);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .concept-panel h3 {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2vw, 1.75rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
  }

  .flow-step,
  .schema-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 0.8rem;
    padding: 0.8rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border, #ddd);
  }

  .flow-step > span {
    display: grid;
    flex: 0 0 2rem;
    width: 2rem;
    height: 2rem;
    place-items: center;
    border: 1px solid currentColor;
    border-radius: 50%;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
  }

  .flow-step div {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.2rem;
  }

  .flow-step small,
  .panel-note {
    color: var(--muted, #666);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  .schema-row {
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .schema-row span {
    color: var(--muted, #666);
  }

  .schema-row strong {
    max-width: 65%;
    text-align: right;
  }

  .panel-note {
    margin-top: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .outcome-note {
    max-width: 75ch;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--muted, #666);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    font-size: 0.92em;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .hero-visual img {
      height: 360px;
    }

    .concept-panel {
      min-height: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
